      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243066/assets/6ec04fe0ad996f87dbc07b0883afb3fa7aef1429412a550afe9102d1c22db71b.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ and passes through the point $(5,-8).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-3.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+8 & =-3(x-5) \\\\ y+8 & =-3x+15 \\\\ y & =-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3 \\\\ y=-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-3 & =-3x+7 \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =7+3 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,-2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(5,-8)$ and $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-5)^{2}+(-2-(-8))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}.$",
