      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x & =-2 \\\\ y & =-2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243066/assets/789659bd700bc5f8407d409bc28bf5ce5309369af2aa5ad5973434d47cff74fa.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-2x-2$ and passes through the point $(-3,-6).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x-2 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -2x-\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-4.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=-4.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(1,-4).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-3,-6)$ and $(1,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-3))}^{2}+(-4-(-6))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2$ is $2\\sqrt{5}.$",
