    {
      "dom_id": "question-74439",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243066/assets/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$",
